    "updated_at": "2023-05-09T16:21:47Z",
    "body": "> Even though non-JavaScript module scripts don't have descendants, we should not lump this logic together with JavaScript and instead have it be generic so it can be shared with future module scripts that do have descendants (e.g., Wasm).\r\n\r\nOK I've adjusted the PR to hoist the R-P parsing and assignment logic outside of the \"If JavaScript\" block. It will only kick in for other modules of we ever decide to use script fetch options for them, of course.\r\n\r\n### Proposed commit message:\r\n\r\n(for once we get a WebKit signal and tests written)\r\n\r\n```\r\nThis commit modifies the module script fetching process to store the parsed\r\n`Referrer-Policy` response header of a module script as the script fetch\r\noptions's referrer policy used for descendant module script fetches. This takes\r\neffect for all recursive module descendants in a graph, and matches the\r\nbehavior for CSS style sheets as specified by the Referrer Policy Standard in\r\nhttps://w3c.github.io/webappsec-referrer-policy/#integration-with-css.\r\n```",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
